<p data-start=3D"3390" data-end=3D"3409" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"3=
390" data-end=3D"3407">Explication :</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"3410" data-end=3D"3709">
<li data-start=3D"3410" data-end=3D"3537" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"3412" data-end=3D"3537" class=3D"">La classe <code data-st=
art=3D"3422" data-end=3D"3429">Forme</code> est abstraite et d=C3=A9finit l=
a m=C3=A9thode <code data-start=3D"3466" data-end=3D"3486">calculer_surface=
()</code> que toutes ses sous-classes doivent impl=C3=A9menter.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"3538" data-end=3D"3619" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"3540" data-end=3D"3619" class=3D""><code data-start=3D"354=
0" data-end=3D"3548">Cercle</code> et <code data-start=3D"3552" data-end=3D=
"3563">Rectangle</code> impl=C3=A9mentent cette m=C3=A9thode selon leur pro=
pre logique.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"3620" data-end=3D"3709" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"3622" data-end=3D"3709" class=3D"">On peut traiter diff=C3=
=A9rents objets <code data-start=3D"3656" data-end=3D"3663">Forme</code> de=
 mani=C3=A8re uniforme gr=C3=A2ce au polymorphisme.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start=3D"3711" data-end=3D"3757" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
3715" data-end=3D"3755">2.2 Avantages des Classes Abstraites</strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"3758" data-end=3D"3997" class=3D"">=E2=9C=85 Fournissent u=
ne base commune et =C3=A9vitent la duplication de code.<br data-start=3D"38=
23" data-end=3D"3826">
=E2=9C=85 Permettent un certain degr=C3=A9 de r=C3=A9utilisation en incluan=
t des m=C3=A9thodes concr=C3=A8tes.<br data-start=3D"3908" data-end=3D"3911=
">
=E2=9C=85 Assurent que toutes les sous-classes impl=C3=A9mentent certaines =
m=C3=A9thodes essentielles.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"3999" data-end=3D"4002" class=3D"">
<h2 data-start=3D"4004" data-end=3D"4050" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
4007" data-end=3D"4048">3. Les Interfaces et le Polymorphisme</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start=3D"4052" data-end=3D"4092" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
4056" data-end=3D"4090">3.1 D=C3=A9finition d'une Interface</strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"4094" data-end=3D"4257" class=3D"">Une <strong data-start=
=3D"4098" data-end=3D"4111">interface</strong> est une structure qui d=C3=
=A9finit un contrat que les classes impl=C3=A9mentant cette interface doive=
nt respecter. Contrairement aux classes abstraites :</p>
<ul data-start=3D"4258" data-end=3D"4523">
<li data-start=3D"4258" data-end=3D"4334" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"4260" data-end=3D"4334" class=3D"">Elle ne contient que de=
s <strong data-start=3D"4285" data-end=3D"4308">m=C3=A9thodes abstraites</s=
trong> (pas d=E2=80=99impl=C3=A9mentation).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4335" data-end=3D"4416" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"4337" data-end=3D"4416" class=3D"">Elle ne peut pas conten=
ir d'attributs d'instance (uniquement des constantes).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4417" data-end=3D"4523" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"4419" data-end=3D"4523" class=3D"">Une classe peut impl=C3=
=A9menter plusieurs interfaces, permettant une forme de polymorphisme plus =
flexible.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h4 data-start=3D"4525" data-end=3D"4578" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
4530" data-end=3D"4551">Exemple en Python</strong> (simulant une interface)=
</h4>
<p data-start=3D"4580" data-end=3D"4710" class=3D"">Python n'a pas de suppo=
rt natif pour les interfaces comme Java ou C#, mais on peut utiliser une cl=
asse abstraite pour les simuler.</p>
